%n išassi | ištar | kīma ālitti
#note: See @bib{RN117@886}: “It is customary to take @akk{{d}iš-tar} as a proper noun. However, the following line, which develops the idea further, shows that the mother goddess is the subject here. Though Ištar and Bēlet-ilī can be identified in the more syncretistic theological traditions (@i{CT} 25 30, 12), they are normally quite separate deities. Ištar is quite out of place as the lamenting goddess on this occasion. The parallel passage of OB Atram-ḫasīs has a similar couplet with @i{il-tum} in the first line and @akk{{d}ma-mi} in the second (III iii 32-3); thus I take @akk{{d}iš-tar} as a common noun, anticipating @i{bēlet-ilī} (cf. Bottero,₂ p. 191, fn. 2; for another example in SB Gilgameš see SB I 274, where @akk{{d}}@i{ištari ummīšu}, ‘the goddess, his mother’, is Ninsun). The word @i{ištaru}, feminine and singular, provides a stark contrast with @i{ilū}, masculine and plural, in the previous line. The compassionate reaction of the individual most affected by the disaster is set against the selfish reaction of the crowd. The mother goddess initiates the lamentation for, as she will herself emphasize, it is her offspring who have been destroyed. The phrase @i{kīma ālitti} provides an advance clue to the goddess’s identity, for Bēlet-ilī is the archetypal female in childbirth. MS [NinNA2]’s @i{ma-li-ti} is ostensibly a different word, but since this is a root @i{primae}-w it cannot be excluded that it is an orthographic variant only, akin to @i{mar-šu-ti} for @i{aršūti} in SB VI 3.”
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" b+32

%n unambi | bēlet-ilī || ṭābat | rigma
