%n ušēṣī-ma | ana erbet (|) šārī || attaqi | niqâ(ti)
#note: See @bib{RN117@890}, “(…) @i{šūṣû} means to fetch something out of something (as with the birds in ll. 148-54, and the table in SB VIII 215), and here describes the preparations for the sacrifice. The winds, which symbolize the four corners of the earth, are thus not the directions in which the boat’s cargo disperses but those in which the sacrifices are made (so also Lieberman and CAD N/1, p. 339, though otherwise Š/2, p. 136).” See also @bib{RN602@420–421}, who believes, with most previous interpreters, that the direct object of @i{ušēṣī-ma} are “die Bootsinsassen (…), die sich in alle vier Winde zerstreuen: ‘Ich ließ (sie) aussteigen …’.”
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" d+30

%n ilū | annûtu | lū uqnî | kišādīya
#note: See @bib{RN117@891}: “The couplet’s syntax has caused difficulties (the most recent exegesis is by @bib{NABU1998-30}). It is best understood in the light of the parallel OB Atram-ḫasīs III vi 2-4, where a nominal clause modified with precative @i{lū} is followed by a clause with the voluntative @i{luḫsus}: @akk{zu-ub-bu-u₂ a[n-nu-tum] lu-u₂ uq-ni ki-sa-di-i[a-a-ma] | lu-uh-su₂-us₂-ma u₄-mi [an-nu-tim] zi-[...]}, ‘these flies [shall be] the lapis lazuli (beads) around my neck, so that I remember [these] days […]’ (cf. W. von Soden, @i{TUAT} III/4, p. 643). (…) The uncertainty of the sources regarding the verb of the second line has led previous commentators to read @i{aḫsusamma}, with or without @i{lū}, but neither form makes sense (@i{lū} with the preterite denotes retrospective wish, ‘I should have …’, or reports past fact, ‘I did …’). Reference to the OB text encourages me instead to read MS [NinNA1] as @akk{lu-u₂-uh-su-sa-am-ma}, an orthography comparable with the same tablet’s @akk{lu-u₂-up-te} in SB XI 281 (note also @akk{lu#-u#-uš-pu-uk} in SBVI 30, Kuyunjik MS). The other manuscripts’ @i{aḫsusamma} is corrupt.”
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" e+2-e+3

