%n ayyānum-ma | uṣi | napištu
#note: See @bib{RN117@891}: “As Lambert remarked in his note on the parallel couplet of Atram-ḫasīs (OB III vi 9-10), the older text’s @akk{a-ia-a-nu} indicates that MS [AššNA1] (which is better restored @akk{[a-a-n]u}- than Borger’s @akk{[man]-nu}-, @bib{RN52aa@110}) has the better tradition of reading in this line, since it avoids the conflict of gender between @i{ayyumma} and @i{napištu}. The same manuscript also confirms that ll. 175-6 are a couplet, not a single line.” On the version of the line in OB @i{Atramḫasīs}, see @bib{NABU2017-43@43}.
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" e+9

%n ay ibluṭ | mamman | ina karāši
#note: See @bib{RN117@891}: “The vetitive @i{ay ibluṭ} normally (if not always) denotes wish in the present-future, ‘may he not survive’, though in this line it is nevertheless conventionally translated as if referring to the past (e.g. von Soden, @i{Reclam}⁵: ‘überleben sollt’ niemand’). Further research is needed to discover whether the negation of past @i{lū ibluṭ}, ‘would that he had survived’, can really be @i{ay ibluṭ} and not, as expected, @i{lū lā ibluṭ} (cf. in form positive and negative wish with the stative, @i{lū damqat} : @i{lū lā damqat}).” See also @bib{RN602@421}: “@i{aj} + Präteritum ist das negative Gegenstück für den irrealen Wunsch der Vergangenheit. Für das positive Gegenstück @i{lū} + Präteritum vgl. SB XI 119.” The transcription of @akk{LU₂} as @i{mamman} follows @bib{NABU2008-78@78}.
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" e+10

%n ninurta | pâšu || īpuš-ma | iqabbi
#note: See @bib{RN117@891 ad 177–182}: “Cf. OB Atram-ḫasīs III vi 11-17, where, however, there are two differences: first, it is Anu who suggests to Enlil the culprit’s identity. The reason for his substitution by Ninurta is not clear. Second, Enki addresses his reply to ‘the great gods’, i.e. all the gods in assembly.”
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" e+11

%n ēa | pâšu || īpuš-ma | iqabbi
#note: On the parallel lines from @i{Atramḫasīs} for XI 181–205, see @bib{RN1023@195–201} and @bib{RN707@157-158 and n. 25}.
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" c+16

%n rummi | ay ibbatiq || šudud | ay irmu
#note: See @bib{RN117@891–892}: “cf. OB Atram-ḫasīs III vi 24: @akk{[...] u₃ ru-um-mi}. In trying to make this line fit the specific context of the punishment of sinful mankind, most translators have followed the spirit of A. L. Oppenheim’s rendering: ‘but be careful lest (an innocent) might be punished, act gently that an(other) might not [come to harm]!’ (@bib{RN729@55, with fn. 2}: ‘the verbs @i{ramû} II and @i{šadādu} have here the nuance ‘to go slow, to let loose’). I can find no support for this. While @i{šadādu} can be ‘to heed’, even without @i{ina}/@i{ana libbi} (as in SB XII 32) and @i{rummû} can be ‘to relax’ (trans.), so far from being synonyms they are more commonly antonyms, ‘to pull taut’ and ‘to slacken, loosen’; and @i{ay ibbatiq} has nothing to do with punishment. Heidel translates more accurately (‘let loose, that he shall not be cut off; pull tight, that he might not ge[t (too) loose]’, but still identifies the anonymous third person singular with ‘man’. I do not find this convincing. In my view the line is proverbial, using the imagery of hauling a boat upstream (@i{šadādu} is the usual verb for this work). The point is that the appropriate amount of force must be applied: too much, and the rope will snap under the boat’s inertia; too little, and momentum will be lost as the line goes slack. Enlil’s retribution has been out of all proportion to what was required, and Ea goes on to list the less dire means that are suitable for the reduction of human numbers.”
// cf. L I.1 OB "III" e+24

%n ammaku | taškunu | abūba
#note: See @bib{RN117@892}: “These lines, and the six that follow, are conventionally translated as if the precatives @i{litbâmma} and @i{liššakinma} were @i{lū itbâmma} and @i{lū iššakinma}, in other words, as retrospective wishes. The Deluge is certainly past (@i{taškunu}) but this does not allow us to ignore the grammar and force the alternative means of reducing the population into the past also. Ea uses the precative because the alternatives remain at Enlil’s disposal and should be used in future.” But compare @bib{RN602@421}: “Es ist deshalb von Soden und Hecker zuzustimmen, daß sich diese Aussage auf eine erneute, künftige Flut beziehen muß, und das Präteritum @i{taškunu} folglich die Vorzeitigkeit in der Zukunft ausdrückt: “anstatt daß du eine Flut gemacht haben wirst” = “anstatt daß du eine Flut machen wirst”.”
// L I.4 SB "XI" 190
// L I.4 SB "XI" 192
// L I.4 SB "XI" 194

