%n iṣbat | qātīyā-ma || uštēlânni | yâši
#note: See @bib{RN117@892}: “(…) It does, indeed, make better sense if Enlil removes Ūta-napišti and his wife from the ark before spiriting them away. That way they are blessed and immortalized in full view of the gods whose number they join.”
// cf. L I.1 NB "-" a+16

%n ilput | pūtnī-ma | izzaz | ina birīni | ikarrabannâši
#note: See @bib{RN117@892–893}: “(…) The repetition of the syllable /@i{put}/ is perhaps intended to suggest the pat of Enlil’s hand on the foreheads of Ūta-napišti and his wife. Enlil’s action is also reported in the new fragment of Atra-ḫasīs, MMA 86.11.378A rev. v 21: @akk{[i]l-pu-ut pu-ta u pu-us-s[a]}, ‘he touched my forehead and her forehead’. Lambert (@bib{RN1023@201}) comments that ‘this ceremony was no doubt based on a custom in human society, perhaps the OB rite of freeing a slave’, comparing an OB expression used in those circumstances, @i{pūtam ullulum}, lit. ‘to render the forehead pure’.”
// cf. L I.1 NB "-" a+21

%n harrān (|) illika || litūr | ina šulmi
#note: See @bib{RN117@893 ad 217–218}: “This couplet distantly echoes the words of the prayers and valedictions made at the beginning of Gilgameš’s heroic career (OB III 214-15, SB III 29-30). This may be an intentional signal that at last his journey is over. (…)”
// cf. L I.4 SB "III" 29

%n gana | epî | kurummātīšu || šitakkanī | ina rēšīšu
#note: See @bib{RN117@893}: “The verb @i{šitakkanī} (with @i{ištakkan} in l. 223) is an unambiguous example of the serial nuance of the iterative infix /tan/ found especially with verbs of placing: the subject puts things in position in turn, or one by one, to form a row of them (see @i{GAG}³ §91f). A comparable use if the I/3 stem of the same verb occurs in an inscription of Nebuchadnezzar II in which he sets in position a row of cedar logs to form the deck of a bridge (@akk{ši-tak-ka-an}: @bib{george1988babylonian@149, 26}).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 223

%n šanītu | muššukat || šaluštu | raṭbat
#note: See @bib{RN117@893}: “It is conventional to derive @akk{muš-šu-kat₃} from @i{mussuku},which means ‘to be ugly, foul, vile’. According to the attestations of this verb and associated words in the dictionaries, this root never uses /š/ as its middle radical. Semantically it is not obviously appropriate. Its most common use is of tarnished reputation and it is not found with foodstuffs. Bread in any case does not putrefy in the open: it goes mouldy and hard. Thus I feel emboldened to posit as a denominative verb @i{muššuku} ‘to turn (something) into leather’, for bread, particularly Mesopotamian bread, goes leathery as it dries out. (It is only proper to note that long after reaching this conclusion I found that the translation of @i{muššukat} as ‘leathery’ already occurs in @bib{gardner_gilgamesh._1985@241 and 245}.)
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 238

%n sebūtu | ina pēttim-ma | ilpussū-ma | i(te)ggeltâ | amēlu
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 216
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 241

%n malêšu | ina mê || kīma elli | limsi
#note: @bib{RN117@894}: “(…) I compare the idiom with @i{Maqlû} III 70: @akk{e-te-lil ki-ma nam-ru}. There is no class of priest known as @i{namru} and the phrase seems to mean ‘I have become as pure as pure can be’. The commentary on this line offers the explanation @akk{nam-ru}= @akk{{d}ša₂-maš}, ‘pure = sun’ (@i{KAR} 94, 44), but this explanation is not convincing and probably represents a late scholar’s ad hoc rendering of an old idiom that had perhaps fallen out of use. Previous commentators have failed to notice that Sumerian possesses a similar construction, as seen in the stock phrase @sux{hul₂.la.gim im.ma.na.ni.ib.gar} in Gudea’s hymn on cylinders (Cyl.A xiv 5-6, xvii 28, xx 4, 12), translated as ‘it made him extremely happy’, in my view correctly, by D. O. Edzard, @i{RIME} 3/I, pp. 77–81. In a building inscription of Samsuiluna the same Sumerian phrase relates the pleasure felt by Šamaš at the destiny Enlil has decreed for Sippar. An Akkadian translation is extant: @akk{ki-ma hi-du-tim it-ta-aš-ka-an-šum} (Frayne, @i{RIME} 4, p. 376, 23–4//31–2). This looks very much like a mechanical rendering of a construction that was not properly understood. I conclude that both languages, Akkadian and Sumerian, can express the superlative by construing an adjective with @i{kīma} // @sux{gim}” (see also @bib{RN7871@231–232}). @bib{RN602@422} considers this interpretation “gewagt” and translates instead “Sein Filzhaar wasche er mit Wasser wie ein (kultisch) Reiner” (following @i{CAD} E 106a). Note, however, that constructions of @i{kī} + Substantive or Adjective are known since the Middle Babylonian period (@bib{RN27201@§114f}; @bib{RN2787@202}; @bib{jimenez2022middle@86}), and that this meaning would fit the context: “let him wash his matted hair clean.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 263

%n ṭāba | ṣupu | zumuršu
#note: @bib{RN602@422} advocates for an interpretation of @akk{ṣu₂#-pu#} as stative D, instead of imperative.
// cf. L I.4 SB "XI" 265

%n mīnâ | tattannaššum-ma || itâr | ana mātīšu
#note: @bib{RN117@894}: “The form @i{tattannaššu} is perfect not, as most translators have it, present-future. The assimilation is typical of MB and later dialects (see @i{CAD} N/1, p. 44). Ūta-napišti’s repetition of his wife’s words in the same tense (l. 280) is by way of exclamation, a rhetorical question begging the answer ‘nothing’.”
